WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.595
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:18.107
Unit eleven: Medicine and the Future. In this unit, you will discuss emerging medical technologies and their implications.

4
00:00:19.117 --> 00:00:20.571
precision medicine.

5
00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:26.916
precision medicine means treatment tailored to a patient's genes, environment and lifestyle.

6
00:00:27.216 --> 00:00:30.318
Precision medicine targets cancer more effectively.

7
00:00:31.018 --> 00:00:34.088
Your turn. Say: precision medicine.

8
00:00:36.788 --> 00:00:38.300
precision medicine.

9
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:40.353
telemedicine.

10
00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:45.397
telemedicine means medical care provided remotely by video or phone.

11
00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:48.846
Telemedicine reaches patients in remote provinces.

12
00:00:49.546 --> 00:00:52.547
Your turn. Say: telemedicine.

13
00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:56.364
telemedicine.

14
00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.013
regenerative medicine.

15
00:00:59.713 --> 00:01:04.406
regenerative medicine means repairing or replacing damaged tissues and organs.

16
00:01:04.706 --> 00:01:08.842
Regenerative medicine may one day grow new organs.

17
00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:13.332
Your turn. Say: regenerative medicine.

18
00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:17.590
regenerative medicine.

19
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:20.200
longevity science.

20
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.860
longevity science means research into extending healthy human life.

21
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.727
Longevity science attracts billions in investment.

22
00:01:30.427 --> 00:01:34.484
Your turn. Say: longevity science.

23
00:01:37.184 --> 00:01:38.789
longevity science.

24
00:01:39.783 --> 00:01:40.889
biohacking.

25
00:01:41.589 --> 00:01:46.003
biohacking means self-experimentation to improve the body or mind.

26
00:01:46.303 --> 00:01:49.232
Biohacking ranges from diets to risky implants.

27
00:01:49.932 --> 00:01:52.305
Your turn. Say: biohacking.

28
00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:56.065
biohacking.

29
00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.794
clinical AI.

30
00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:05.337
clinical AI means artificial intelligence used to support medical diagnosis.

31
00:02:05.637 --> 00:02:09.354
Clinical AI can detect eye disease in seconds.

32
00:02:10.054 --> 00:02:13.601
Your turn. Say: clinical AI.

33
00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:17.383
clinical AI.

34
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:20.174
wearable diagnostics.

35
00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:25.788
wearable diagnostics means devices worn on the body that monitor health.

36
00:02:26.088 --> 00:02:29.283
Wearable diagnostics can warn of heart problems.

37
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:33.541
Your turn. Say: wearable diagnostics.

38
00:02:36.241 --> 00:02:37.962
wearable diagnostics.

39
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.427
pharmacogenomics.

40
00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:45.878
pharmacogenomics means the study of how genes affect responses to medicines.

41
00:02:46.178 --> 00:02:51.045
Pharmacogenomics explains why drugs work differently for different people.

42
00:02:51.745 --> 00:02:55.988
Your turn. Say: pharmacogenomics.

43
00:02:58.688 --> 00:03:00.107
pharmacogenomics.

44
00:03:01.117 --> 00:03:03.256
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:03.556 --> 00:03:07.903
Rithy, imagine medicine in 2050. What do you see?

46
00:03:09.217 --> 00:03:13.945
Treatment designed for you, by you, around you: precision medicine.

47
00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.878
A neat tricolon! Will clinical AI replace doctors?

48
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:24.350
Replace them? No. Assist them? Absolutely.

49
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:30.195
And longevity science? Some promise we'll live to a hundred and fifty.

50
00:03:31.483 --> 00:03:35.764
Live longer, perhaps. But live better? That's the real question.

51
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.550
What about biohacking?

52
00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:45.463
It's not medicine; it's marketing. Not every experiment is progress.

53
00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:50.305
Grammar focus: Rhetorical devices in argument.

54
00:03:50.605 --> 00:03:53.557
Rhetorical devices make arguments memorable.

55
00:03:53.857 --> 00:04:01.730
A tricolon: designed for you, by you, around you. Hypophora: Replace them? No. Assist them? Absolutely.

56
00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.108
Antithesis: It's not medicine; it's marketing.

57
00:04:06.408 --> 00:04:13.585
Rhetorical devices make arguments memorable; use them to highlight key claims, not to replace evidence.

58
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:18.002
Now read and listen. The Medicine of Tomorrow.

59
00:04:18.302 --> 00:04:23.227
Medicine is entering an era of extraordinary technological change.

60
00:04:24.517 --> 00:04:29.442
Precision medicine promises treatments tailored to each patient's genetic profile.

61
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:36.604
Pharmacogenomics already helps doctors predict how patients will respond to certain drugs.

62
00:04:37.917 --> 00:04:42.714
Telemedicine and wearable diagnostics are bringing care into homes and villages.

63
00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:50.300
Clinical AI can analyse scans faster than any human, though it still requires human oversight.

64
00:04:51.617 --> 00:04:57.924
Regenerative medicine and longevity science raise the prospect of repairing ageing bodies.

65
00:04:59.217 --> 00:05:05.500
Biohacking, by contrast, often blurs the line between innovation and risky self-experimentation.

66
00:05:06.817 --> 00:05:12.406
Will these advances serve everyone, or only those who can afford them? That is the defining question.

67
00:05:13.717 --> 00:05:16.485
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:16.785 --> 00:05:19.435
Medical care provided remotely is blank.

69
00:05:19.735 --> 00:05:25.048
A, telemedicine. B, biohacking. C, regenerative medicine.

70
00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.955
The answer is A: telemedicine.

71
00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:37.481
'Veni, vidi, vici' is an example of a blank.

72
00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:42.131
A, tricolon. B, antithesis. C, euphemism.

73
00:05:46.117 --> 00:05:48.128
The answer is A: tricolon.

74
00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:53.056
'It's not a cost; it's an investment' uses blank.

75
00:05:53.356 --> 00:05:57.834
A, antithesis. B, hypophora. C, tricolon.

76
00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.488
The answer is A: antithesis.

77
00:06:05.983 --> 00:06:09.817
The study of how genes affect responses to medicines is blank.

78
00:06:10.117 --> 00:06:16.244
A, pharmacogenomics. B, longevity science. C, clinical AI.

79
00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.271
The answer is A: pharmacogenomics.

80
00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:28.791
According to the text, what does clinical AI still require?

81
00:06:29.091 --> 00:06:34.649
A, human oversight. B, no doctors. C, more patients.

82
00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.358
The answer is A: human oversight.

83
00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:50.077
Now it's your turn. Part one: Will new medical technologies make healthcare fairer or more unequal?

84
00:06:55.377 --> 00:06:59.942
Part two: Use at least two rhetorical devices in a short argument.

85
00:07:06.242 --> 00:07:19.569
Fairer or more unequal? That depends on policy, not technology. Telemedicine can reach the village, the farm and the floating community. But innovation without access is not progress; it is privilege.

86
00:07:19.869 --> 00:07:22.426
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:23.417 --> 00:07:28.809
Great work today! You learned eight new words and rhetorical devices in argument.

88
00:07:29.109 --> 00:07:33.793
Today's value is healthy body. Move your body every day.

89
00:07:34.093 --> 00:07:36.792
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

90
00:07:37.092 --> 00:07:39.628
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

